/* data/learn-c/phy-12-14.js - GENERATED by tools/build_learn.py from data/learn/summaries/phy-12-14.json (our own words;
 * the notes of one chapter, loaded when the chapter is opened). Do not edit by hand. */
(window.LEARN_C = window.LEARN_C || {})["phy-12-14"] = {"14.1":{"s":"Light behaves as a wave in interference, diffraction and polarisation. But black body radiation and the photoelectric effect cannot be explained that way. Planck proposed that energy is given out in packets (quanta) of size hν, so higher frequency means bigger packets. This chapter shows that radiation and matter both have a wave side and a particle side.","f":[{"x":"E = n*h*nu","n":"Planck: an oscillator gives out energy only in whole packets of h nu; n = 1, 2, 3 ...","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 14.1, p.306"}],"x":["In interference, diffraction and polarisation, light behaves as a wave. But some facts, like the spectrum of a hot body and the photoelectric effect, cannot be explained with waves alone. In 1900, Planck said that energy is given out and taken in only in small packets, called quanta. A quantum of radiation of frequency ν has the energy E = hν. Here h is Planck's constant, 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ J s.","So the energy of an oscillator can only be 0, hν, 2hν, 3hν, and so on, and never a value in between. A high frequency means a big packet.","This chapter follows the idea in two steps. First, radiation, which seems to be a wave, also behaves as a stream of particles. Second, matter, which seems to be made of particles, also behaves as a wave. This two-sided nature is called wave-particle duality.","Common mistake: thinking that a photon is a tiny ball with a mass. It has energy and momentum, but its rest mass is zero."],"e":"Find the energy of one quantum of light of frequency 5 × 10¹⁴ Hz. E = hν = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ × 5 × 10¹⁴ = 3.3 × 10⁻¹⁹ J, which is about 2.1 eV."},"14.2":{"s":"Light of high enough frequency knocks electrons out of a metal surface. Emission starts at once, needs a frequency above a threshold that depends on the metal, and the electrons' greatest kinetic energy rises with frequency but not with intensity; intensity only changes the current. Wave theory cannot explain this. Einstein: light comes as photons of energy hν; one photon gives all its energy to one electron, which spends the work function to escape.","f":[{"x":"E = h*nu = h*c/lambda","n":"Energy of one photon; h = Planck's constant = 6.63 × 10^-34 J s.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 14.2, p.311"},{"x":"nu_0 = phi_0/h","n":"Threshold frequency; phi_0 = work function of the metal.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 14.2, p.311"},{"x":"KE_max = h*nu - phi_0","n":"Einstein's photoelectric equation.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 14.2, p.312"},{"x":"e*V_0 = h*nu - phi_0","n":"V_0 = stopping potential: the reverse voltage that just stops the fastest electrons.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 14.2, p.313"},{"x":"V_0 = (h/e)*nu - phi_0/e","n":"Stopping potential against frequency is a straight line of slope h/e, the same for every metal.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 14.2, p.313"}],"w":["Brighter light gives more electrons (more current), not faster ones.","Below the threshold frequency no electrons come out, however bright the light."],"x":["When light of a high enough frequency falls on a metal, electrons are ejected from the surface. This is the photoelectric effect, and the electrons are called photoelectrons. The experiments show: (1) there is a threshold frequency ν₀ for each metal, and below it, no electrons come out, however bright the light is; (2) the emission begins at once, with no delay; (3) the maximum kinetic energy of the electrons rises with the frequency, and does not depend on the intensity; (4) the intensity of the light decides the number of electrons, so the current.","The wave theory cannot explain this. Einstein's explanation: light comes as photons, each with energy hν. One photon gives all its energy to one electron. The electron uses the work function φ₀, the least energy needed to leave the metal, and keeps the rest as kinetic energy: KE_max = hν − φ₀. The threshold frequency is ν₀ = φ₀/h.","To measure KE_max, apply a reverse voltage. The stopping potential V₀ is the reverse voltage that just stops the fastest electrons: eV₀ = KE_max. A graph of V₀ against ν is a straight line with the slope h/e, the same for all metals.","Common mistake: thinking that more intense light gives faster electrons. It gives more electrons, not faster ones."],"e":"A metal has a work function of 2.0 eV. Light of photon energy 3.1 eV falls on it. KE_max = 3.1 − 2.0 = 1.1 eV, and the stopping potential is 1.1 V. Light of photon energy 1.8 eV gives no electrons at all."},"14.3":{"s":"Light shows wave behaviour (interference, diffraction) in some experiments and particle behaviour (photoelectric effect, Compton scattering) in others; we need both pictures. Compton found that X-rays scattered by electrons come out with a longer wavelength, as in a collision of particles: photons carry momentum as well as energy. A photon always moves at the speed of light and has zero rest mass.","f":[{"x":"Delta lambda = (h/(m_0*c))*(1 - cos(theta))","n":"Compton shift; theta = scattering angle, m_0 = electron's rest mass. Does not depend on the incident wavelength.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 14.3, p.315"}],"w":["In photon collisions energy and momentum are conserved, but the number of photons is not."],"x":["Light shows wave behaviour in interference and diffraction, and particle behaviour in the photoelectric effect and in Compton scattering. Both pictures are needed. Which one shows up depends on the experiment.","A photon has the energy E = hν = hc/λ. It moves with the speed of light and has zero rest mass. Its momentum is p = E/c = h/λ. So a photon of a short wavelength carries more momentum.","Compton's experiment: X-rays were scattered by electrons, and the scattered X-rays had a longer wavelength than the incident ones. This is just what happens when two particles collide. The photon loses some energy and momentum to the electron, so its wavelength increases. The shift is Δλ = (h/m₀c)(1 − cos θ), where θ is the angle of scattering and m₀ is the rest mass of the electron.","The shift does not depend on the incident wavelength. It is about 2.4 pm at 90°, so it is noticeable only with short waves like X-rays. Common mistake: forgetting that in a photon collision, the number of photons is not conserved."],"e":"Find the momentum of a photon of wavelength 500 nm. p = h/λ = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴/(500 × 10⁻⁹) = 1.33 × 10⁻²⁷ kg m/s."},"14.4":{"s":"A photocell uses the photoelectric effect to turn light into an electric current. A curved photosensitive cathode and a wire anode sit in an evacuated glass or quartz bulb with a battery and a microammeter. When suitable light falls on the cathode, a current flows; when the light is cut off, it stops. Uses: light meters, automatic street lights, burglar alarms and counters.","w":["A solar cell is not a photocell of this kind: it makes a voltage in a semiconductor (photovoltaic effect)."],"x":["A photocell is a device that changes light into an electric current. It uses the photoelectric effect.","It has a curved cathode, coated with a metal that gives photoelectrons easily, and a wire anode. Both are in a glass or quartz bulb from which the air has been removed. The cathode and the anode are joined to a battery and a microammeter. When light of a suitable frequency falls on the cathode, electrons come out and are collected by the anode, so a current flows. When the light is cut off, the current stops.","The current is proportional to the intensity of the light, as long as the frequency is above the threshold. For a quartz window, ultraviolet light can pass, and this lets a cell work with metals that need a higher frequency.","Uses: light meters in cameras, switching on street lights when it gets dark, burglar alarms, door openers and counters. Common mistake: confusing it with a solar cell. A solar cell makes a voltage inside a semiconductor, and it works in a different way."],"e":"In a burglar alarm, a light beam falls on a photocell. When a person cuts the beam, the photocurrent stops. A circuit notices this and rings the alarm."},"14.5":{"s":"De Broglie proposed that, since radiation behaves as both wave and particle, moving matter should too. A particle with momentum p has a matter wave of wavelength h/p. The larger the momentum, the shorter the wavelength. For a charged particle accelerated from rest through a potential difference V, its kinetic energy is qV, which gives the wavelength directly.","f":[{"x":"p = E/c = h/lambda","n":"Momentum of a photon.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 14.5, p.317"},{"x":"lambda = h/(m*v)","n":"De Broglie wavelength of a particle of mass m and speed v.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 14.5, p.317"},{"x":"lambda = h/sqrt(2*m*q*V)","n":"Charged particle q accelerated from rest through V (not near light speed).","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 14.5, p.317"},{"x":"lambda = 1.228/sqrt(V) nm","n":"Electron accelerated through V volts.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 14.5, p.317"}],"w":["p = E/c is only for photons; for an electron use p = mv or the kinetic energy.","For the same kinetic energy a heavier particle has a shorter wavelength."],"x":["De Broglie argued that if radiation can behave as a particle, then moving matter should be able to behave as a wave. A particle of momentum p has a matter wave of wavelength λ = h/p = h/mv. The bigger the momentum, the shorter the wavelength.","The photon is a special case: p = E/c = h/λ. Do not use p = E/c for an electron or any other particle with a mass. For them, use p = mv, or find p from the kinetic energy K: p = √(2mK).","For a charged particle q, accelerated from rest through a potential difference V, K = qV, so λ = h/√(2mqV). For an electron this works out as λ = 1.228/√V nm, with V in volts. For 100 V, it is 0.123 nm, about the size of the spacing between atoms in a crystal.","For the same kinetic energy, a lighter particle has a longer wavelength, as λ is proportional to 1/√m. Common mistake: using the speed of light for the speed of an electron. Matter waves do not move at c."],"e":"An electron is accelerated through 25 V. λ = 1.228/√25 = 0.2456 nm. If the voltage is made 4 times larger, 100 V, then λ halves to 0.123 nm."},"14.6":{"s":"Davisson and Germer fired electrons from an electron gun at a nickel crystal and measured how many were scattered at each angle. Instead of an even spread they found a strong peak, at 50° for 54 V electrons, caused by interference of waves scattered from layers of atoms. The measured wavelength (about 0.165 nm) agreed with de Broglie's 1.228/√54 ≈ 0.167 nm, proving that electrons behave as waves.","w":["Electron wavelengths are a few ångström, the size of atomic spacing, which is why a crystal works as the grating."],"x":["In 1927 Davisson and Germer tested de Broglie's idea. They fired a beam of electrons from an electron gun at a nickel crystal. A detector measured the number of electrons scattered at different angles.","If electrons were only particles, they would be scattered in all directions about evenly. But they found a strong peak at one angle. For electrons accelerated through 54 V, the peak was at an angle of 50° to the beam. This is a diffraction pattern, made by waves scattered from the layers of atoms in the crystal, which work as a diffraction grating.","From the spacing of the atoms and the angle, they found the wavelength to be about 0.165 nm. De Broglie's formula, 1.228/√54, gives 0.167 nm. The two agree very well, and this was the first proof that electrons behave as waves.","Electron waves have wavelengths of the same size as the spacing between atoms, which is why a crystal works for them. Later, electron diffraction was used to study the structure of materials, and the electron microscope uses the wave nature of electrons."],"e":"Electrons accelerated through 54 V have λ = 1.228/√54 = 0.167 nm. The measured value in the experiment, 0.165 nm, differs from it by about 1 percent."},"14.7":{"s":"All moving matter has a wave nature, just as radiation has a particle nature. The wave side only shows up for very small objects, because h is tiny: a running child or a moving car has a de Broglie wavelength far smaller than anything around it, so no diffraction is seen. For electrons and atoms the wavelength matches atomic sizes and wave effects become important.","w":["Everyday objects do have a wavelength; it is just far too small to notice (about 10⁻³⁴ m or less; 6.6 × 10⁻³⁶ m for a running student)."],"x":["Every moving object has a matter wave with wavelength λ = h/mv. But Planck's constant is very small, 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ J s. So for an ordinary object, the wavelength is far too small to notice.","A ball of mass 0.15 kg moving at 20 m/s has λ = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴/3 = 2.2 × 10⁻³⁴ m. That is much smaller than an atom, and nothing around us has gaps of this size, so there is no diffraction. A running person has a wavelength of the order of 10⁻³⁶ m.","For an electron or an atom, the mass is tiny, so the wavelength is of the same size as the atomic spacing, and wave effects are important. This is why matter waves matter in atoms, but not in cricket.","So the wave-particle duality applies to everything: radiation and matter both have a wave side and a particle side. Common mistake: saying that a big object has no wave. It does have one, but it is too small to detect."],"e":"Compare the wavelengths at the same speed, 10⁶ m/s. For an electron (9.1 × 10⁻³¹ kg): λ = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴/(9.1 × 10⁻²⁵) = 7.3 × 10⁻¹⁰ m. For a dust grain of 10⁻⁹ kg: λ = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴/(10⁻⁹ × 10⁶) = 6.6 × 10⁻³¹ m, far too small to detect."}};
